{"kind": "completion", "decision": "continue", "cursor": 201, "request_id": "completion-3", "message": "**Target 2 (JSON Theme Support) - CONTRACT VIOLATION**\n\nThe requirement explicitly states:\n\"Color resolution order: variant-specific colors are checked first, then generic `\"Colors\"`, then the default theme fallback\"\n\n**Current implementation flaw:**\n\nThe jsonTheme struct has only ONE colors map:\n```go\ntype jsonTheme struct {\n    colors map[fyne.ThemeColorName]color.Color  // Single map for all colors\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nAll three JSON sections (Colors, Colors-dark, Colors-light) write to this SAME map during parsing (lines 130-165). This means:\n- If Colors defines \"background\": \"#ffffff\"\n- And Colors-dark defines \"background\": \"#000000\"  \n- They OVERWRITE each other in the same map\n\nThe Color() method ignores the variant parameter entirely:\n```go\nfunc (t *jsonTheme) Color(name fyne.ThemeColorName, variant fyne.ThemeVariant) color.Color {\n    if c, ok := t.colors[name]; ok {\n        return c  // Returns whatever was last written, no variant check\n    }\n    return DefaultTheme().Color(name, variant)\n}\n```\n\n**What's required:**\nSeparate storage for generic vs. dark vs. light colors, so Color() can:\n1. Check variant-specific map first (Colors-dark if variant==Dark, Colors-light if variant==Light)\n2. Then check generic Colors map\n3. Then fall back to default theme\n\nCurrently there's no way to distinguish between generic and variant-specific colors at lookup time, making variant-specific color support non-functional.", "control_submission": true, "submission_id": "completion-3", "delivery_task_turn": 101, "delivery_cursor": 201, "delivery": "handed_to_completion_boundary"}
{"kind": "completion", "decision": "continue", "cursor": 235, "request_id": "completion-4", "message": "**Target 3 (Data Binding Formatting) - BEHAVIORAL CONTRACT VIOLATION**\n\nThe sprintf binding implementation in `data/binding/sprintf.go` exists with correct structural signatures but **does not implement the required behavior** specified in the task requirements.\n\n## Contract Requirements (task/original_task.txt lines 88-96)\n\n1. **NewSprintf Set() behavior**: \"Set(str string) parses the string back using fmt.Sscanf and sets each source binding to the parsed value\"\n   - Special handling: Bytes cannot round-trip (returns error), URI parsed via storage.ParseURI\n\n2. **NewSprintf source types**: \"variadic DataItem arguments (which may be Bool, Bytes, Float, Int, Rune, String, or URI bindings)\"\n\n3. **StringToStringWithFormat optimization**: \"If the format is `\"%s\"` (identity), return the original binding directly (no wrapping)\"\n\n## Current Implementation Violations\n\n**sprintf.go line 34**: Set() always returns \"sprintf binding is read-only\"\n```go\nfunc (s *sprintfBinding) Set(string) error {\n    return fmt.Errorf(\"sprintf binding is read-only\")\n}\n```\n❌ Should implement reverse parsing with fmt.Sscanf\n\n**sprintf.go lines 23-24**: Get() assumes all sources are String\n```go\nval, err := source.(String).Get()\n```\n❌ Should support Bool, Bytes, Float, Int, Rune, String, URI using type switches\n\n**sprintf.go lines 156-162**: StringToStringWithFormat always creates wrapper\n```go\nfunc StringToStringWithFormat(source String, format string) String {\n    s := &stringToStringWithFormatBinding{\n        format: format,\n        source: source,\n    }\n    return s\n}\n```\n❌ Should check `if format == \"%s\" { return source }` before wrapping\n\nThe sprintf binding must implement:\n- Bidirectional parsing (Get formats with Sprintf, Set parses with Sscanf)\n- Support for all DataItem types (Bool, Bytes, Float, Int, Rune, String, URI)\n- Identity optimization for \"%s\" format", "control_submission": true, "submission_id": "completion-4", "delivery_task_turn": 118, "delivery_cursor": 235, "delivery": "handed_to_completion_boundary"}
